✅ 4. Determining the Number of Clusters — Elbow Method

The Elbow method is used to choose the optimal value of K.

Procedure

Run K-means for different values of K (e.g., 1–10).

Compute WCSS (Within-Cluster Sum of Squares) for each K.

Plot K on x-axis vs WCSS on y-axis.

The “elbow point” is where the decrease in WCSS becomes gradual, forming a bend.

This K is chosen as the optimal number of clusters.

Interpretation

Too few clusters → high WCSS (bad segmentation)

Too many clusters → overfitting, no clear patterns

The elbow balances performance and simplicity.

✅ 5. Hierarchical Clustering (Agglomerative)
Concept

Hierarchical clustering builds a tree-like structure (dendrogram) showing how data points merge into clusters.

Types

Agglomerative (Bottom-up):
Each point starts as its own cluster → progressively merged

Divisive (Top-down):
Entire data → repeatedly split

Agglomerative is used in most practical cases.

Algorithm Steps

Treat each sample as a separate cluster.

Compute distance matrix between all clusters.

Merge the two closest clusters based on linkage criteria.

Update the distance matrix and repeat until one cluster remains.

Linkage Methods

Single linkage → min distance

Complete linkage → max distance

Average linkage → mean distance

Ward’s linkage → minimizes variance (best for numeric data)

Output

Dendrogram

Optimal cluster count determined by cutting dendrogram at a height where natural grouping appears.

✅ 6. Comparison Between K-Means and Hierarchical Clustering
Feature	K-Means	Hierarchical
Type	Partitional	Tree-structured
Speed	Fast for large datasets	Slower (O(n²))
Clusters	Requires pre-deciding K	Determined from dendrogram
Data size	Efficient for large N	Suitable for small–medium datasets
Output	Cluster centers	Dendrogram for full structure
✅ 7. Practical Output / Results Discussion

After running the algorithms:

K-Means

WCSS decreases rapidly at first

Elbow typically around K = 3 or 4 for sales data

Clusters group orders/products by sales volume, quantity, and price

Hierarchical Clustering

Dendrogram shows groups visually

Cutting at a reasonable height gives similar clusters (often 3–4 groups)

✅ 8. Viva Questions & Answers
Q1: Why do we scale the data before clustering?

Because clustering uses distances; unscaled features bias the result.

Q2: What is WCSS?

Within-Cluster Sum of Squares – total variance within clusters.

Q3: Why is the elbow method used?

To determine the optimal cluster count where adding more clusters gives minimal improvement.

Q4: Difference between K-Means and Hierarchical?

K-Means is fast and needs K beforehand; hierarchical is slower but visually informative.

Q5: What is a dendrogram?

A tree diagram showing how clusters merge at different distances.

Q6: Why can K-Means give different results on different runs?

Because it initializes centroids randomly.
